    {
      "cve": "CVE-2022-37660",
      "ids": [
        {
          "system_name": "SUSE CVE Page",
          "text": "https://www.suse.com/security/cve/CVE-2022-37660"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "category": "general",
          "text": "In hostapd 2.10 and earlier, the PKEX code remains active even after a successful PKEX association. An attacker that successfully bootstrapped public keys with another entity using PKEX in the past, will be able to subvert a future bootstrapping by passively observing public keys, re-using the encrypting element Qi and subtracting it from the captured message M (X = M - Qi). This will result in the public ephemeral key X; the only element required to subvert the PKEX association.",
          "title": "CVE description"
        }
      ],
      "product_status": {
        "recommended": [
          "openSUSE Tumbleweed:hostapd-0:2.12-1.1.aarch64",
          "openSUSE Tumbleweed:hostapd-0:2.12-1.1.ppc64le",
